-- title:
--   Theorem 6.6 — arbitrary advice distributions replaced by samplers of description complexity $2^{\ell_P+\ell_V}\mathrm{poly}(T,\log(1/\varepsilon))$
-- statement:
--   Fix $\lambda$, $x$ and a one-query reduction $\mathcal R$ interacting with a challenger $\mathcal C$, with queries in $\{0,1\}^{\ell_V}$ and replies in $\{0,1\}^{\ell_P}$, where $\ell_V, \ell_P \le T$. Let $\mathcal D_{x,\lambda}$ be any distribution on advice strings of length at most $T$, sampled independently of all other coins. For every $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$ there is a distribution $\widetilde{\mathcal D}_{x,\lambda}$, generated by a sampler of description complexity
--   $$
--   2^{\ell_P + \ell_V}\,\mathrm{poly}\bigl(T, \log(1/\varepsilon)\bigr),
--   $$
--   such that replacing $z \leftarrow \mathcal D_{x,\lambda}$ by $z \leftarrow \widetilde{\mathcal D}_{x,\lambda}$ changes the winning probability of $\mathcal R^{\mathcal A}(1^\lambda, x, z)$ against $\mathcal C(1^{f(1^\lambda, |x|)})$ by at most $\varepsilon$, for every possibly unbounded randomized response oracle $\mathcal A$. The replacement distribution does not depend on $\mathcal A$. The polynomial is universal.
--
--   This removes the description-complexity hypothesis on the advice of a one-query reduction (Corollary 6.7), at a cost exponential only in the lengths of the single query and its reply.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The sampler is a circuit code in the sense of Definition 4.15 (the published `IsSamplerCode`). $\mathrm{poly}(T, \log(1/\varepsilon))$ is $(T + \lceil\log_2(1/\varepsilon)\rceil + 2)^c$ with one exponent $c$ chosen before everything else. The oracle $\mathcal A$ answers every query, with replies of exactly $\ell_P$ bits.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: a one-query T-time reduction → a reduction that makes no oracle query after a reply on any view at the fixed (λ, x); no running-time hypothesis; reason: the statement uses T only to bound ℓ_V, ℓ_P and the advice length, which stay hypotheses; every one-query T-time reduction satisfies the Lean hypothesis
--   - encoding: queries in {0,1}^{ℓ_V} → every query the reduction makes at (λ, x), for every advice string, coins and view, has length ℓ_V; reason: the page's condition on the reduction at (λ, x)
--   - encoding: a distribution D̃ generated by a sampler of description complexity 2^{ℓ_P+ℓ_V} poly(T, log(1/ε)) → ∃ c chosen before all data, a circuit code of length ≤ 2^{ℓ_P+ℓ_V}·(T + ⌈log₂(1/ε)⌉ + 2)^c that samples D̃ exactly (IsSamplerCode); reason: the poly convention of the mission; Definition 4.15 for description complexity
--   - encoding: every possibly unbounded randomized response oracle A with replies in {0,1}^{ℓ_P} → every A : List Bool → PMF (List Bool) whose replies all have length ℓ_P, answering each query with fresh coins; reason: stateless randomized oracle of the published execution law
-- source:
--   Jain, Mathialagan, Roy, From Succinct Arguments to Proofs: Lower Bounds for Obfuscation and SNARGs, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 1af78662…), Theorem 6.6, p.42 (PDF 45)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LaconicBarrier_Reduction_winProb
import Definitions.Def_LaconicBarrier_HasDescription

namespace LaconicBarrier.Supplement

/-- **Theorem 6.6** (Jain, Mathialagan, Roy, *From Succinct Arguments to Proofs: Lower Bounds for
Obfuscation and SNARGs*, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 1af78662…), Theorem 6.6, p.42):
arbitrary advice distributions can be replaced by samplers of bounded description complexity. Fix
`λ`, `x` and a one-query reduction `R` interacting with a challenger `C`, with queries in
`{0,1}^{ℓ_V}` and replies in `{0,1}^{ℓ_P}`, where `ℓ_V, ℓ_P ≤ T`. Let `D_{x,λ}` be any distribution on
advice strings of length at most `T`, sampled independently of all other coins. For every
`ε ∈ (0, 1)` there is a distribution `D̃_{x,λ}` generated by a sampler of description complexity
`2^{ℓ_P+ℓ_V} poly(T, log(1/ε))` such that replacing `z ← D_{x,λ}` by `z ← D̃_{x,λ}` changes the
winning probability of `R^A(1^λ, x, z)` against `C(1^{f(1^λ,|x|)})` by at most `ε` for every possibly
unbounded randomized response oracle `A` (with replies in `{0,1}^{ℓ_P}`). The replacement is
independent of `A`.

**Formalization Note** One polynomial for everything: the exponent `c` comes before the game, the
reduction, `λ`, `x`, the bounds, the distribution and `ε`; `poly(T, log(1/ε))` is
`(T + ⌈log₂(1/ε)⌉ + 2)^c`. The sampler is a published `IsSamplerCode` (Definition 4.15). The
reduction's one-query property and its query length are required at the fixed `(λ, x)` only, and
its time bound is not assumed (the statement does not use it). `A` is the same stateless oracle
for every query (`winProb` with `fun _ => A`). -/
theorem theorem_6_6 : ∃ c : ℕ, ∀ (G : Game) (R : Reduction) (lam : ℕ) (x : List Bool)
    (T ℓV ℓP : ℕ), ℓV ≤ T → ℓP ≤ T →
    (∀ (z : List Bool) (ρ : Fin (R.coins lam x.length) → Bool) (evs : List Ev) (q : List Bool),
      1 ≤ evs.countP Ev.isReply → R.next lam x z ρ evs ≠ .query q) →
    (∀ (z : List Bool) (ρ : Fin (R.coins lam x.length) → Bool) (evs : List Ev) (q : List Bool),
      R.next lam x z ρ evs = .query q → q.length = ℓV) →
    ∀ D : PMF (List Bool), (∀ z ∈ D.support, z.length ≤ T) →
    ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ε < 1 →
    ∃ (Dt : PMF (List Bool)) (code : List Bool), IsSamplerCode code Dt ∧
      code.length ≤ 2 ^ (ℓP + ℓV) * (T + ⌈Real.logb 2 (1 / ε)⌉₊ + 2) ^ c ∧
      ∀ A : List Bool → PMF (List Bool), (∀ (q : List Bool), ∀ a ∈ (A q).support, a.length = ℓP) →
        |R.winProb G (fun _ => A) lam x D - R.winProb G (fun _ => A) lam x Dt| ≤ ε := by sorry

end LaconicBarrier.Supplement
